Figure 2a,b shows the \(I{-}V\) characteristics of the MOF-device as a function of incident laser power densities \((P)\) at \(785~\mathrm{nm}\) at 300 and \(77\,\mathrm{K}\), respectively. Both curves reveal an increase in the photocurrent with increasing photon density, demonstrating the operation of the active MOF layer as a photoconductor at both temperatures. In order to precisely interrogate the effect of temperature on the performance of the photodetector, we measured the photoresponse of the sample in the temperature range of \(77{-}300\ \mathrm{K}\) under various fluences of \(785~\mathrm{nm}\) illumination. Figure 2c shows current (at a bias of \(-1\;\mathrm{V}\)) as a function of

inverse temperature \((1/T)\) measured in dark (black curve) and under different \(785\,\mathrm{nm}\) light intensities ranging from 0.026 (red curve) to 0.60 (orange curve) \(\mathrm{W~cm}^{-2}\) \((I{-}V\) curves measured in dark in the temperature range of \(77{-}300\ \mathrm{K}\) are shown in Figure S2, Supporting Information). The obtained trend is consistent with the narrow IR bandgap of the samples, enabling thermally activated charge carrier population of the conduction and valence bands at higher temperatures (in agreement with our previous findings, where the \(1.7\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) thick sample revealed a thermally activated carrier density population, described by \(N\propto\exp[-E_{\mathrm{g}}/2k T]\). From Hall measurements, a charge density of \(6.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) was obtained at \(300\ \mathrm{K}\), which decreased to \(2.0\times10^{11}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) at \(100\ \mathrm{K}\), ref. [17]). On fitting the exponential function, an activation energy of \(0.35\pm0.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) is extracted from \(I\) versus \(1/T\) plot, which is comparable to the optical bandgap of \(0.45\ \mathrm{eV}\) resolved for the samples. At higher temperatures \((T\ \geq\ 200\ \mathrm{K})\), the increase of thermally induced electronic transitions across the narrow bandgap results in larger dark currents, overshadowing the detection of optically generated

